      "content": "Benchmarking the operational efficacy of a local coding agent model deployed on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 requires a granular dissection of hardware constraints, software optimizations, and the inherent tradeoffs between latency, throughput, and semantic fidelity. The RTX 4090, with its 24GB of GDDR6X memory and approximately 284 TOPS of FP16 tensor core performance, represents the current apex of consumer-grade inference hardware, yet it imposes strict boundaries on model size and context window management. When evaluating a local coding agent, the primary metric shifts from raw perplexity to end-to-end task completion time, which is heavily influenced by speculative decoding mechanisms, prompt caching strategies, and the efficiency of long-context prefilling. The following analysis delves into these components, examining how Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) speculative decoding interacts with the 4090’s memory bandwidth, how prompt caching mitigates redundant computation in iterative coding workflows, and how the model handles the structural rigidity of JSON and tool-use formatting while maintaining high-quality patch generation.\n\nThe RTX 4090’s 24GB VRAM is the critical bottleneck for local coding agents. Modern large language models optimized for code, such as variants of Llama-3-70B quantized to 4-bit or 8-bit, or smaller dense models like Qwen2.5-Coder-32B, must fit entirely within this memory budget to avoid CPU offloading, which would introduce catastrophic latency spikes. For a 70B parameter model at 4-bit quantization, the model weights alone consume approximately 35-40GB, necessitating aggressive quantization or model sharding, which degrades performance. Consequently, the sweet spot for a fully VRAM-resident agent on a 4090 lies in the 14B to 32B parameter range, particularly when using high-efficiency quantization formats like GGUF Q4_K_M or AWQ. These models offer a compelling balance between reasoning capability and inference speed. However, the context window adds significant memory pressure. A 128K context window for a 32B model can require an additional 10-15GB of VRAM for the KV cache, leaving little headroom for batch processing or dynamic allocation. This constraint forces a rigorous evaluation of memory management strategies, particularly prompt caching, which becomes not just an optimization but a necessity for interactive coding sessions.\n\nMulti-Token Prediction (MTP) speculative decoding is a transformative technique for local inference, particularly on hardware with high compute-to-memory bandwidth ratios like the RTX 4090. Traditional autoregressive decoding generates one token at a time, creating a sequential bottleneck where the GPU spends significant time idle while waiting for the next token’s probability distribution to be computed. MTP addresses this by training the model to predict multiple future tokens simultaneously. In practice, a small draft model or the main model’s auxiliary heads generate a sequence of candidate tokens, which are then verified in parallel by the main model. On an RTX 4090, this parallel verification leverages the tensor cores’ ability to process multiple tokens concurrently, effectively amortizing the computational cost of the large model over several tokens. Benchmarks indicate that with a well-tuned draft model, MTP can achieve acceptance rates of 60-80% for coding tasks, resulting in a 2-3x speedup in tokens per second (TPS). However, the benefit is contingent on the draft model’s accuracy. If the draft model is too small or poorly aligned with the main model’s distribution, the verification step fails frequently, negating the speedup and adding overhead. For coding agents, where token distributions are often skewed",
